Now, we know that <imath>\left[CDE\right] + \left[ABE\right] -\left[ABC\right] = \left[ ADE\right]= 12.5</imath>. Therefore,
<cmath>\frac{5\cdot(5-x)}{2} + \frac{7}{2} - \frac{7 - \sqrt{x^2-10x+50}}{2}=12.5</cmath>
<cmath>5\cdot(5-x) + 7 - (7 - \sqrt{x^2-10x+50})=25</cmath>
<cmath>25-5x +  \sqrt{x^2-10x+50}=25</cmath>
<cmath>\sqrt{x^2-10x+50}=5x</cmath>
<cmath>x^2-10x+50=25x^2</cmath>
<cmath>0=24x^2+10x-50</cmath>
<cmath>0=12x^2+5x-25</cmath>
<cmath>0=(4x-5)(3x+5)</cmath>
Now, we know that <imath>x</imath> must be positive so our only valid solution is
<cmath>x=\frac{5}{4}</cmath>
Finally, we can substitute this value of <imath>x</imath> into our expression for length <imath>\overline {CB}</imath>.  The other leg would just be <imath>1</imath> making for a total area of <imath>\frac{1}{2} \cdot\overline{CB}</imath>. Hence,
<cmath>\left[ ABC\right]= \frac{7 - \sqrt{x^2-10x+50}}{2}</cmath>
<cmath>\left[ ABC\right]= \frac{7 - \sqrt{\frac{5}{4}^2-10\cdot \frac{5}{4}+50}}{2}</cmath>
<cmath>\left[ ABC\right]= \frac{7 - \sqrt{\frac{25}{16}-\frac{50}{4}+\frac{200}{4}}}{2}</cmath>
<cmath>\left[ ABC\right]= \frac{7 - \sqrt{\frac{25}{16}+\frac{150}{4}}}{2}</cmath>
<cmath>\left[ ABC\right]= \frac{7 - \sqrt{\frac{25}{16}+\frac{600}{16}}}{2}</cmath>
<cmath>\left[ ABC\right]= \frac{7 - \sqrt{\frac{625}{16}}}{2}</cmath>
<cmath>\left[ ABC\right]= \frac{7 - \frac{25}{4}}{2}</cmath>
<cmath>\left[ ABC\right]= \frac{\frac{28}{4} - \frac{25}{4}}{2}</cmath>
<cmath>\left[ ABC\right]= \frac{\frac{3}{4}}{2}</cmath>
<cmath>\left[ ABC\right]=\frac{3}{8} \implies \boxed{\textbf{(A)} \frac{3}{8}}</cmath>
~Shockfront99

== Solution 11 ==
Connect <imath>AE</imath>, we can find that <imath>AE=\sqrt{AB^2+BE^2}=\sqrt{1^2+7^2}=5\sqrt{2}</imath>.
And <imath>\angle ADE=90^\circ</imath>, <imath>AE=5\sqrt{2}</imath>, <imath>AD=5</imath>, then we can get <imath>ED=5</imath>, <imath>\triangle AED</imath> is an isosceles right triangle 

Then, <imath>\angle AED=45^\circ</imath>
Let <imath>\angle CED=\angle \alpha</imath>, <imath>\angle \alpha=\angle AED-\angle AEC</imath>

Because of the tangent subtraction formula <imath>\tan(\alpha-\beta)=\frac{\tan\alpha-\tan\beta}{1+\tan\alpha\tan\beta}</imath>, we can get
<imath>\tan\alpha=\frac{\tan\angle AED-\tan\angle AEC}{1+\tan\angle AED\cdot\tan\angle AEC}</imath>

Since <imath>\tan\angle AED=\tan 45^\circ=1 </imath>, and <imath>\tan\angle AEC=\frac{AB}{EB}=\frac{1}{7}</imath>, we can get
<imath>\tan\alpha=\frac{3}{4}</imath>

Because <imath>\frac{CD}{ED}=\tan\alpha=\frac{3}{4}</imath>, then <imath>CD=\frac{15}{4}</imath>, so <imath>EC=\frac{25}{4}</imath>.

Thus, <imath>BC=\frac{3}{4}</imath>.

So, the area of <imath>\triangle ABC=\frac{1}{2}\cdot BC\cdot AB=\frac{1}{2}\cdot\frac{3}{4}=\frac{3}{8}=\boxed{A}</imath>.

~lyrawjs

== Solution 12 (Similar to Solution 3) ==

We notice that by vertical angles that <imath>\angle DCE = \angle BCA</imath>, and we also have <imath>\angle CDE = \angle ABC = 90</imath>. <imath>\triangle BCA</imath> is similar to <imath>\triangle DCE</imath> by Angle-Angle Similarity.
Now let <imath>BC = x</imath>. Using the Pythagorean Theorem on <imath>\triangle ABC</imath>, we have <imath>CA = \sqrt{x^2 + 1}</imath>. Since <imath>ABEF</imath> is a rectangle, we have <imath>AF = 7 = BE</imath>, which also means <imath>CE = 7 - x</imath>. Since <imath>AD = 5</imath>, <imath>DC = 5 - \sqrt{x^2 + 1}</imath>. From our similar triangles, we have <imath>\frac{BC}{DC} = \frac{CA}{CE} \Longrightarrow \frac{x}{5 - \sqrt{x^2 + 1}} = \frac{\sqrt{x^2 + 1}}{7-x}</imath>. Cross multiplying, simplifying, and squaring gives us <imath>7x - x^2 = 5\sqrt{x^2 + 1} - x^2 - 1 \Longrightarrow 7x + 1 = 5\sqrt{x^2 + 1} \Longrightarrow 49x^2 + 14x + 1 = 25x^2 + 25 \Longrightarrow 12x^2 + 7x - 12 = 0</imath>. We can use the quadratic formula, and take the positive solution. <imath>x = \frac{-7 + \sqrt{7^2 - 4(12)(-12)}}{2(12)} = \frac{3}{4}</imath>. Since ABC is a right triangle, the area is <imath>\frac{1}{2} (AB)(BC) = \frac{1}{2} (1)(\frac{3}{4}) = \boxed{\textbf{(A)} \frac{3}{8}}</imath>, which is our final answer.

~BlazeSai2

